    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete and green.\n\n## Summary\n\nMoved date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts 1 July, numbered by the calendar year it ends in \u2014 Australian convention, e.g. an Aug-2026 date is FY27).\n\n**New \u2014 `util/FiscalYear.java`**: central fiscal-year logic derived from a date.\n- `yearOf(date)` \u2014 July\u2013Dec \u2192 `year+1`, Jan\u2013June \u2192 `year`.\n- `labelOf(date)` \u2014 `FY<YY>` (zero-padded last two digits).\n- `elapsedYears(from, to)` \u2014 whole fiscal years between two dates.\n\n**`fiscalYear` response field**:\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `fiscalYear` property (`FY<YY>`) to the `Owner` schema.\n- `OwnerMapper` \u2014 maps `fiscalYear` from the stored (business-day-adjusted) `registrationDate` via `FiscalYear.labelOf`, null-safe.\n\n**Membership identifier \u2014 `AssignMembershipNumber`**: the `-M<YY>` year segment now uses the fiscal year of the adjusted registration date instead of the plain calendar year. Verified behaviour change in the audit log: an Aug-2026 registration now yields `\u2026-M27` (was `-M26`).\n\n**Tenure \u2014 `MembershipLevel`**: the +3 tenure points now trigger on *elapsed fiscal years* (`FiscalYear.elapsedYears(registration, today) > 1`) rather than a 365-day window. Constant renamed `TENURE_DAYS`\u2192`TENURE_FISCAL_YEARS`; dropped the `ChronoUnit.DAYS` computation.\n\nDescriptions in `openapi.yml` for `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` were updated to match.\n\n**Tests**: `AcceptanceTest.coreReturnsFiscalYear` (asserts `fiscalYear` matches `FY\\d{2}`) passes; full suite `BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec fixes the format (`FY<YY>`) and the fiscal-year start (1 July) but not the labelling convention or the exact tenure threshold. I used the ending-year convention (which is what makes the membership-number behaviour visibly change for current dates, as the task requires) and kept the tenure threshold at \"more than one\" fiscal year to mirror the original \">1 year\" intent. Both are documented in the code and OpenAPI descriptions; easy to flip if you intended the start-year convention or a `>= 1` threshold.",
